<rdf:RDF
  xmlns:atlas="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/"
  xmlns:owl="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#"
  xmlns:rdfs="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#"
  xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/"
  xmlns:skos="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#"
  xmlns:cco="https://www.commoncoreontologies.org/"
  xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#"
>
  <owl:AnnotationProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/displayContext">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">display context</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The span of the verbatim that is the author's own plain-language reading
of the display form — Klir's "where S, T, R denote…", Bunge's disjointness clause. Same contract
as atlas:displayForm: an exact substring of atlas:verbatim, enforced by the build. Together the
two spans let an interface lead with the mathematics and follow with the author's gloss without
one editorial word being added.</skos:definition>
  </owl:AnnotationProperty>
  <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/refuses">
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/FormalSystemDefinition"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/Example"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">refuses</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Relates a definition to a case its author says is NOT a system on it.
The separating-instance data: where two definitions rule differently on the same case is where
the loss catalogue comes from.</skos:definition>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">NOT EVERY NEGATIVE IS A REFUSAL. Bunge refuses ontically -- no bonds,
therefore not a system, and no act of description alters it. Klir's "a collection of books is
not a system, only a set" is not a refusal at all: on his criterion an object is a system iff
it CAN BE DESCRIBED as conforming, and a bare collection can be. Recording the second as a
refusal would manufacture a symmetry the authors do not share. See
docs/proposals/P3-stance-axes.md.</rdfs:comment>
  </owl:ObjectProperty>
  <atlas:FormalSystemDefinition rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/entry/bunge-1979-ces-triple">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Bunge (1979), general characterisation: σ = ⟨C, E, S⟩</rdfs:label>
    <atlas:statedIn>
      <cco:ont00000253 rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/bearer/bunge-1979-treatise-vol4">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Bunge, M. — Treatise on Basic Philosophy, Vol. 4: Ontology II — A World of Systems (Reidel, 1979)</rdfs:label>
        <dcterms:creator>Mario Bunge</dcterms:creator>
        <dcterms:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#gYear">1979</dcterms:date>
        <dcterms:identifier>doi:10.1007/978-94-009-9392-1</dcterms:identifier>
      </cco:ont00000253>
    </atlas:statedIn>
    <atlas:sourceLocation>ch. 1, §1.1 (the general concept of a system)</atlas:sourceLocation>
    <atlas:verbatim>One way of characterizing the general concept of a system is this. Let T be a nonempty set. Then the ordered triple σ = ⟨C, E, S⟩ is (or represents) a system over T iff C and E are mutually disjoint subsets of T (i.e. C ∩ E = ∅), and S is a nonempty set of relations on the union of C and E. The system is conceptual if T is a set of conceptual items, and concrete (or material) if T ⊆ Θ is a set of concrete entities, i.e. things.</atlas:verbatim>
    <atlas:displayForm>σ = ⟨C, E, S⟩</atlas:displayForm>
    <atlas:displayContext>C and E are mutually disjoint subsets of T (i.e. C ∩ E = ∅), and S is a nonempty set of relations on the union of C and E.</atlas:displayContext>
    <atlas:authorCaveat>However, the preceding is not a definition proper, because it does not tell us what exactly is the membership of the coordinates C, E and S of the ordered triple. We must therefore define the notions of composition, environment, and structure of a thing.</atlas:authorCaveat>
    <atlas:admits>
      <atlas:Example rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/case/bunge-theory">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">a theory</rdfs:label>
        <atlas:gloss>a theory: 𝒯 = ⟨P, B, ⊢⟩ — propositions P, embedded in a body of knowledge B, glued by entailment ⊢</atlas:gloss>
        <atlas:sourceLocation>ch. 1, §1.1, p. 5</atlas:sourceLocation>
        <atlas:evidenceCode rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/HVP"/>
        <atlas:encodedBy>Shingai Thornton (Claude-assisted)</atlas:encodedBy>
        <atlas:encodedOn rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-08-01</atlas:encodedOn>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Bunge's own worked conceptual-system example, given inline in §1.1 rather than in a labelled Example block. Location human-checked against the source 2026-08-05.</rdfs:comment>
      </atlas:Example>
    </atlas:admits>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/composition">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">composition</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Bunge's C. The set of a thing's parts, relativised to a class A and a time t.</skos:scopeNote>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/environment">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">environment</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Bunge's E. Things outside the composition that act on, or are acted on by, components.</skos:scopeNote>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/structure">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">structure</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Bunge's S. Relations among components and between components and environment, partitioned into bonding and nonbonding.</skos:scopeNote>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/set">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">set</skos:prefLabel>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/relation">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">relation</skos:prefLabel>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/thing">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">thing</skos:prefLabel>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:formalisedAs>Systems/Category/ShapeBunge.lean#BungeShape</atlas:formalisedAs>
    <atlas:evidenceCode>
      <atlas:EvidenceCode rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/HVP">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">human-verified against primary text</rdfs:label>
        <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A human read the primary source and confirmed the verbatim text and the encoding against it.</skos:definition>
      </atlas:EvidenceCode>
    </atlas:evidenceCode>
    <atlas:encodedBy>Shingai Thornton (Claude-assisted)</atlas:encodedBy>
    <atlas:encodedOn rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-08-01</atlas:encodedOn>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">⚑ "is (or represents)". Bunge's own parenthetical hedges between the triple
BEING a system and REPRESENTING one, and he resolves it later in the same chapter
by calling the triple "the minimal model of system σ at time t". This is direct
primary-text evidence on the is-versus-represents question — the distinction the
Lean encodings do not type. Klir's (T,R) IS the system; Bunge's ⟨C,E,S⟩ is a model
OF a thing. Same shape, different ontological status.

⚑ NOT the same triple as the informal gloss. The fuller form Bunge actually works
with is relativised to a class A of things and indexed by time: s_A(σ,t) =
⟨𝒞_A(σ,t), ℰ_A(σ,t), 𝒮_A(σ,t)⟩. Anyone citing "Bunge's CES triple" as a bare
3-tuple is citing the informal version.

⚑ CES, NOT CESM. Mechanism is not a coordinate here. The string "CESM" does not
occur in the 1979 text; mechanism becomes central in later Bunge (see How Does It
Work, 2004). Any citation pairing "1979" with "CESM" is an anachronism.

DELIBERATELY NOT ASSERTED: cco:ont00001808 (is about). Bunge is a realist and
would endorse the reading; asserting it anyway would still break the catalogue's
neutrality, which must not depend on which tradition an entry belongs to.</rdfs:comment>
  </atlas:FormalSystemDefinition>
  <owl:AnnotationProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/excludedExample">
    <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">excluded example</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">DEPRECATED 2026-08-03. Superseded by atlas:refuses onto an atlas:Example. Retained declared so data written against the old shape stays readable.</rdfs:comment>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Something the author explicitly says is NOT a system on this definition. The separating-instance data, captured at tier 0: where two definitions disagree about the same object is exactly where the loss catalogue comes from.</skos:definition>
  </owl:AnnotationProperty>
  <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/admits">
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/FormalSystemDefinition"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/Example"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">admits</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Relates a definition to a case its author offers as a system.</skos:definition>
  </owl:ObjectProperty>
  <atlas:FormalSystemDefinition rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/entry/bertalanffy-1972-set-in-interrelation">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Bertalanffy (1972), The History and Status of General Systems Theory, p. 417</rdfs:label>
    <atlas:statedIn>
      <cco:ont00000253 rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/bearer/bertalanffy-1972-amj">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Von Bertalanffy, L. — The History and Status of General Systems Theory, Academy of Management Journal 15(4) (1972)</rdfs:label>
        <dcterms:creator>Ludwig von Bertalanffy</dcterms:creator>
        <dcterms:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#gYear">1972</dcterms:date>
        <dcterms:identifier>https://www.jstor.org/stable/255139</dcterms:identifier>
        <dcterms:identifier>doi:10.2307/255139</dcterms:identifier>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Academy of Management Journal 15(4), December 1972, pp. 407–426. Special issue: General Systems Theory.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
      </cco:ont00000253>
    </atlas:statedIn>
    <atlas:sourceLocation>p. 417</atlas:sourceLocation>
    <atlas:verbatim>A system may be defined as a set of elements standing in interrelation among themselves and with the environment. This can be expressed mathematically in different ways.</atlas:verbatim>
    <atlas:displayForm>A system may be defined as a set of elements standing in interrelation among themselves and with the environment.</atlas:displayForm>
    <atlas:displayContext>This can be expressed mathematically in different ways.</atlas:displayContext>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/element">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">element</skos:prefLabel>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/set"/>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/relation"/>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/environment"/>
    <atlas:admits>
      <atlas:Example rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/case/bertalanffy-organism">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">an organism</rdfs:label>
        <atlas:gloss>an organism</atlas:gloss>
        <atlas:verbatim>Any organism is a system, that is, a dynamic order of parts and processes standing in mutual interaction</atlas:verbatim>
        <atlas:sourceLocation>General System Theory (1968), p. 209, quoting Bertalanffy 1949a p. 11</atlas:sourceLocation>
        <atlas:evidenceCode rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/MDHC"/>
        <atlas:encodedBy>Shingai Thornton (Claude-assisted)</atlas:encodedBy>
        <atlas:encodedOn rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-08-05</atlas:encodedOn>
        <rdfs:comment>DIFFERENT BEARER FROM THE ENTRY. This ruling is from the 1968 book, while the
entry's definition is from the 1972 article; the case is recorded here because it is
Bertalanffy ruling on a concrete object in his own voice, which the 1972 article never does.

MDHC, AND THE SCOPE OF THE CHECK IS EXACTLY THIS: a human located "Any organism is a system"
on p. 209 of the book on 2026-08-05, so the page and the ruling itself are confirmed. The
remainder of the sentence — "that is, a dynamic order of parts and processes standing in
mutual interaction" — is still as the OCR gives it and has not been read word for word. HVP
needs that last step.

The page number comes from a Kindle edition reporting print-equivalent pagination; the same
edition's p. 56 for eq. (3.1) matches the print index's "mathematical definition, 56-60",
which is the corroboration for trusting its numbering here.

NOT ENCODED AS A REFUSAL: the heap of bricks. Bertalanffy calls heaps the case where a complex
"can be built up, step by step, by putting together the first separate elements" — merely
summative, as against the constitutive characteristics of a Gestalt (1968, ch. 3). That is
architecturally Klir's book-collection and Bunge's ordered-events exclusion, but he never
writes the verdict sentence, and encoding an implication as a ruling would put words in his
mouth. It belongs to the mapping layer.</rdfs:comment>
      </atlas:Example>
    </atlas:admits>
    <atlas:formalisedAs>Systems/Category/ShapeBertalanffy.lean#Bertalanffy72Shape</atlas:formalisedAs>
    <atlas:evidenceCode rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/HVP"/>
    <atlas:encodedBy>Shingai Thornton (Claude-assisted)</atlas:encodedBy>
    <atlas:encodedOn rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-08-05</atlas:encodedOn>
    <rdfs:comment>HVP: the page was read against this transcription on 2026-08-05, including
the two things a paraphrase would smooth away — that it prints "may be defined" rather than
"can be", and that "and with the environment" is really there. The second is the clause that
distinguishes this definition from the 1968 book's, so the whole entry turns on it.

WHY THE 1972 ARTICLE AND NOT THE 1968 BOOK. The book is the canonical
citation, and its ch. 3 (p. 55) gives the same definition verbally — "a complex of interacting
elements" — followed by the differential-equation formalisation, eq. (3.1). The only copy of
the book available to this encoding is an OCR whose ligatures are systematically damaged and
in which **equation (3.1) did not survive at all**. Transcribing a verbatim from it would
violate the catalogue's first discipline. The 1972 article restates the definition in a text
with clean provenance, so it is entered first. The 1968 entry remains wanted, and the pair is
worth having: the definitions differ.

WHAT THE REVISION CHANGED. 1968: "a complex of interacting elements." 1972: "a set of elements
standing in interrelation among themselves AND WITH THE ENVIRONMENT." The environment becomes
constitutive of the definition rather than a later distinction. Encoding both would make that
revision machine-checkable at the shape level — the 1972 shape carries an arrow the 1968 shape
does not.

WHAT IS DELIBERATELY OUT OF THE VERBATIM. The differential-equation material sits on the
following page (p. 418, "a set of n measures, called state variables... a set of n
simultaneous, first-order differential equations"), and its equation is a page image in this
scan, not extractable text. Including that sentence would end the verbatim on a colon pointing
at something the catalogue does not hold. It is a candidate for a second entry from the same
bearer, in the way Bunge's CES triple and Definition 1.1 are two entries from one book.

THE AUTHOR'S OWN HEDGE is inside the verbatim, not paraphrased around it: "This can be
expressed mathematically in different ways." On the same page he names Mesarović, Maccia,
Ashby and Klir as fellow axiomatisers — the founder pointing at two other entries in this
catalogue.</rdfs:comment>
    <atlas:authorCaveat>This can be expressed mathematically in different ways.</atlas:authorCaveat>
  </atlas:FormalSystemDefinition>
  <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/invokesPrimitive">
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/FormalSystemDefinition"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">invokes primitive</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Relates a definition to a term it uses as a primitive. The census payload: which primitives recur across definitions, and which are unique to one.</skos:definition>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Range is skos:Concept, not an OWL class, deliberately. A primitive here is a TERM USED IN A DEFINITION, not a kind of thing in the world. SKOS is about concepts; using it avoids smuggling realism into constructivist traditions.</rdfs:comment>
  </owl:ObjectProperty>
  <atlas:FormalSystemDefinition rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/entry/klir-2001-eq-1-1">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Klir (2001), Facets of Systems Science, eq. (1.1)</rdfs:label>
    <atlas:statedIn>
      <cco:ont00000253 rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/bearer/klir-2001-facets">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Klir, G. J. — Facets of Systems Science, 2nd ed. (Springer, 2001)</rdfs:label>
        <dcterms:creator>George J. Klir</dcterms:creator>
        <dcterms:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#gYear">2001</dcterms:date>
        <dcterms:identifier>ISBN 978-1-4613-5501-4</dcterms:identifier>
        <dcterms:identifier>doi:10.1007/978-1-4615-1331-5</dcterms:identifier>
      </cco:ont00000253>
    </atlas:statedIn>
    <atlas:sourceLocation>ch. 1, eq. (1.1), p. 5</atlas:sourceLocation>
    <atlas:verbatim>S = (T, R),   (1.1)

where S, T, R denote, respectively, a system, a set of things distinguished within S, and a relation (or, possibly, a set of relations) defined on T. Clearly, the thinghood and systemhood properties of S reside in T and R, respectively.</atlas:verbatim>
    <atlas:displayForm>S = (T, R)</atlas:displayForm>
    <atlas:displayContext>where S, T, R denote, respectively, a system, a set of things distinguished within S, and a relation (or, possibly, a set of relations) defined on T.</atlas:displayContext>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/thing"/>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/relation"/>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/set"/>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/thinghood">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">thinghood</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Klir's term. Recorded as its own primitive rather than folded into `thing`, because whether it is distinct is a census question, not a modelling decision.</skos:scopeNote>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/systemhood">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">systemhood</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Klir's term, paired with thinghood.</skos:scopeNote>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:admits>
      <atlas:Example rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/case/klir-books-ordered">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">a collection of books, ordered</rdfs:label>
        <atlas:gloss>a collection of books ordered by authors' names, publication dates, or size</atlas:gloss>
        <atlas:verbatim>any ordering of a set is a relation defined on the set</atlas:verbatim>
        <atlas:sourceLocation>ch. 1, p. 5</atlas:sourceLocation>
        <atlas:instantiates rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/object/ordered-non-bonding"/>
        <atlas:evidenceCode rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/HVP"/>
        <atlas:encodedBy>Shingai Thornton (Claude-assisted)</atlas:encodedBy>
        <atlas:encodedOn rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-08-03</atlas:encodedOn>
      </atlas:Example>
    </atlas:admits>
    <atlas:admits>
      <atlas:Example rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/case/klir-books-partitioned">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">a collection of books, partitioned</rdfs:label>
        <atlas:gloss>a collection of books partitioned by subject, publisher, or language</atlas:gloss>
        <atlas:verbatim>every partition of a set emerges from a particular equivalence relation defined on the set</atlas:verbatim>
        <atlas:sourceLocation>ch. 1, p. 5</atlas:sourceLocation>
        <atlas:evidenceCode rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/HVP"/>
        <atlas:encodedBy>Shingai Thornton (Claude-assisted)</atlas:encodedBy>
        <atlas:encodedOn rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-08-03</atlas:encodedOn>
      </atlas:Example>
    </atlas:admits>
    <atlas:admits>
      <atlas:Example rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/case/klir-population-by-birth">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">a human population, ordered by dates of birth</rdfs:label>
        <atlas:gloss>a human population ordered by dates of birth</atlas:gloss>
        <atlas:verbatim>may be applied not only to other sets of books, but also to sets whose elements are not books</atlas:verbatim>
        <atlas:sourceLocation>ch. 1, p. 5</atlas:sourceLocation>
        <atlas:evidenceCode rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/HVP"/>
        <atlas:encodedBy>Shingai Thornton (Claude-assisted)</atlas:encodedBy>
        <atlas:encodedOn rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-08-03</atlas:encodedOn>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The quotation licenses this case rather than describing it: Klir is making
a point about a RELATION being transferable to a different carrier set, and the human
population is his instance of it. A splice, recorded as one.</rdfs:comment>
      </atlas:Example>
    </atlas:admits>
    <atlas:formalisedAs>Systems/Category/ShapeKlir.lean#KlirShape</atlas:formalisedAs>
    <atlas:evidenceCode rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/HVP"/>
    <atlas:encodedBy>Shingai Thornton (Claude-assisted)</atlas:encodedBy>
    <atlas:encodedOn rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-08-01</atlas:encodedOn>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">VERIFIED: verbatim transcribed from the primary text, not paraphrased.

DELIBERATELY NOT ASSERTED: cco:ont00001808 (is about). Klir is an avowed
constructivist — on his account the relation R is defined by the modeller, not
discovered. Asserting what this definition is *about* would commit the catalogue
to a realist reading Klir does not hold. The neutrality is the point.

EXAMPLES NOTE: Klir gives no excluded example in Bunge's sense. His "a collection
of books is not a system, only a set" (p. 5, same paragraph as the included
examples) is the opening move of an argument whose conclusion is that organising
it *makes* it one, and on his own criterion — an object is a system iff it CAN BE
DESCRIBED in a form conforming to Eq. (1.1) — a bare collection is not refused but
merely not yet distinguished. Recording it as atlas:excludedExample would give it
the same form as Bunge's ontic refusal at Definition 1.1 and manufacture a symmetry
the two authors do not share; in a cases matrix it would read as agreement where
the finding is the opposite. The three included examples are grouped as Klir groups
them: his "etc." marks the variants as instances of a kind, not an enumeration of
cases. Source read 2026-08-03; see docs/proposals/P1-klir-examples.md and
docs/proposals/P3-stance-axes.md.

CENSUS NOTE: Klir's own gloss splits the definition's two slots into named
properties — thinghood in T, systemhood in R. Recorded as separate primitives
rather than folded into `thing` and `relation`, because whether they are distinct
primitives is a question the census should answer, not one the encoding should
prejudge.

ADJACENT MATERIAL, NOT YET ENTERED: Klir's self-assessment on the same page (the
definition is "too general and, consequently, of little pragmatic value" but
"encompasses all other, more specific definitions of systems") is a claim ABOUT
this definition's relation to others. It belongs in the mapping layer, not in
this entry.

NEXT TIER: the dependency quiver (tier 1) for this definition already exists in
Lean as ShapeKlir in systems-science-foundations. The two encodings must be
checked against each other before either is cited.</rdfs:comment>
  </atlas:FormalSystemDefinition>
  <atlas:FormalSystemDefinition rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/entry/bunge-1979-def-1-1">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Bunge (1979), Definition 1.1: concrete system</rdfs:label>
    <atlas:statedIn rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/bearer/bunge-1979-treatise-vol4"/>
    <atlas:sourceLocation>ch. 1, Definition 1.1</atlas:sourceLocation>
    <atlas:verbatim>DEFINITION 1.1 An object is a concrete system iff it is composed of at least two different connected things.</atlas:verbatim>
    <atlas:displayForm>An object is a concrete system iff it is composed of at least two different connected things.</atlas:displayForm>
    <atlas:admits>
      <atlas:Example rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/case/bunge-molecule">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">a molecule</rdfs:label>
        <atlas:gloss>a molecule</atlas:gloss>
        <atlas:verbatim>A molecule, a coral reef, a family and a factory are systems.</atlas:verbatim>
        <atlas:sourceLocation>ch. 1, §1.2, Example following Definition 1.1, p. 7</atlas:sourceLocation>
        <atlas:evidenceCode rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/HVP"/>
        <atlas:encodedBy>Shingai Thornton (Claude-assisted)</atlas:encodedBy>
        <atlas:encodedOn rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-08-05</atlas:encodedOn>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">One of four admitted cases Bunge gives in a single sentence; the shared
verbatim is that sentence. Split from case:bunge-molecule-reef-family-factory 2026-08-05 so
each case can be matched against other authors' rulings.</rdfs:comment>
      </atlas:Example>
    </atlas:admits>
    <atlas:admits>
      <atlas:Example rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/case/bunge-reef">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">a coral reef</rdfs:label>
        <atlas:gloss>a coral reef</atlas:gloss>
        <atlas:verbatim>A molecule, a coral reef, a family and a factory are systems.</atlas:verbatim>
        <atlas:sourceLocation>ch. 1, §1.2, Example following Definition 1.1, p. 7</atlas:sourceLocation>
        <atlas:evidenceCode rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/HVP"/>
        <atlas:encodedBy>Shingai Thornton (Claude-assisted)</atlas:encodedBy>
        <atlas:encodedOn rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-08-05</atlas:encodedOn>
      </atlas:Example>
    </atlas:admits>
    <atlas:admits>
      <atlas:Example rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/case/bunge-family">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">a family</rdfs:label>
        <atlas:gloss>a family</atlas:gloss>
        <atlas:verbatim>A molecule, a coral reef, a family and a factory are systems.</atlas:verbatim>
        <atlas:sourceLocation>ch. 1, §1.2, Example following Definition 1.1, p. 7</atlas:sourceLocation>
        <atlas:evidenceCode rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/HVP"/>
        <atlas:encodedBy>Shingai Thornton (Claude-assisted)</atlas:encodedBy>
        <atlas:encodedOn rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-08-05</atlas:encodedOn>
      </atlas:Example>
    </atlas:admits>
    <atlas:admits>
      <atlas:Example rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/case/bunge-factory">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">a factory</rdfs:label>
        <atlas:gloss>a factory</atlas:gloss>
        <atlas:verbatim>A molecule, a coral reef, a family and a factory are systems.</atlas:verbatim>
        <atlas:sourceLocation>ch. 1, §1.2, Example following Definition 1.1, p. 7</atlas:sourceLocation>
        <atlas:evidenceCode rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/HVP"/>
        <atlas:encodedBy>Shingai Thornton (Claude-assisted)</atlas:encodedBy>
        <atlas:encodedOn rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-08-05</atlas:encodedOn>
      </atlas:Example>
    </atlas:admits>
    <atlas:refuses>
      <atlas:Example rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/case/bunge-states-of-a-thing">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">a set of states of a thing</rdfs:label>
        <atlas:gloss>a set of states of a thing</atlas:gloss>
        <atlas:sourceLocation>ch. 1, §1.2, Example following Definition 1.1, p. 7</atlas:sourceLocation>
        <atlas:evidenceCode rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/HVP"/>
        <atlas:encodedBy>Shingai Thornton (Claude-assisted)</atlas:encodedBy>
        <atlas:encodedOn rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-08-01</atlas:encodedOn>
      </atlas:Example>
    </atlas:refuses>
    <atlas:refuses>
      <atlas:Example rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/case/bunge-events-ordered">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">a collection of events, even if ordered</rdfs:label>
        <atlas:gloss>a collection of events, even if ordered</atlas:gloss>
        <atlas:instantiates rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/object/ordered-non-bonding"/>
        <atlas:sourceLocation>ch. 1, §1.2, Example following Definition 1.1, p. 7</atlas:sourceLocation>
        <atlas:evidenceCode rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/HVP"/>
        <atlas:encodedBy>Shingai Thornton (Claude-assisted)</atlas:encodedBy>
        <atlas:encodedOn rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-08-01</atlas:encodedOn>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Half of the catalogue's first separating instance: Klir admits an ordered
collection, Bunge refuses one. The refusal is ontic — an aggregate is a collection "not held
together by bonds" (p. 4), Definition 1.1 requires the members connected, and Definition
1.2(iii) carries 𝔅≠∅; ordering is not bonding — so he refuses ordered BOOKS for the same
reason. See mappings/ordered-collection.md.

The location was deliberately withheld until human-checked: these cases come from the labelled
Example block that follows Definition 1.1 in §1.2, not from Definition 1.1 itself, so entry
003's location did not cover them. Checked against the source and entered 2026-08-05.</rdfs:comment>
      </atlas:Example>
    </atlas:refuses>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/thing"/>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/part">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">part</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Bunge's part-of relation, written x [ y.</skos:scopeNote>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/bond">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">bond</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">A bonding relation, as opposed to a merely nonbonding one. Bunge requires the bonding set to be nonempty; this is the primitive that does the refusing.</skos:scopeNote>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/action">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">action</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Bunge's acts-on relation, written x |&gt; y.</skos:scopeNote>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:evidenceCode rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/HVP"/>
    <atlas:encodedBy>Shingai Thornton (Claude-assisted)</atlas:encodedBy>
    <atlas:encodedOn rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-08-01</atlas:encodedOn>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">⚑ THE SEPARATING INSTANCE, IN BUNGE'S OWN WORDS. His excluded example is
"a collection of events, EVEN IF ORDERED". Klir, on the passage recorded in entry
001, holds that ordering a collection is exactly what makes it a system: "any
ordering of a set is a relation defined on the set". Two founders, both verbatim,
disagreeing about whether an ordered collection is a system. This is the highest-
value datum in the catalogue so far and it was found by reading, not by inference.

MACHINERY BEHIND THE REFUSAL (Definition 1.2, encoded here as primitives rather
than as its own entry, because it defines composition/environment/structure and
not "system"):
  𝒞_A(σ,t) = {x ∈ A | x ⊏ σ}
  ℰ_A(σ,t) = {x ∈ A | x ∉ 𝒞_A(σ,t) &amp; ∃y (y ∈ 𝒞_A(σ,t) &amp; (x ▷ y ∨ y ▷ x))}
  𝒮_A(σ,t) = relations, in particular bonds, on 𝒞_A(σ,t) ∪ ℰ_A(σ,t), with the
             bonding set 𝔅_A(σ,t) ≠ ∅
The nonempty-bonding requirement is what does the refusing, and it is encoded in
systems-science-foundations as ConcreteSystem.bondage_nonempty over the ActsOn
class (Bunge's ▷). The two encodings agree; that agreement should be recorded as
a mapping claim once the mapping layer exists, not asserted here.

RELATION TO ENTRY 002: Bunge presents 002 first and then disowns it as "not a
definition proper". 003 is the definition proper. They are not alternative
phrasings of one claim and must not be merged.</rdfs:comment>
  </atlas:FormalSystemDefinition>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/entries/bunge-1979-treatise">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Atlas entries — Bunge 1979, Treatise on Basic Philosophy vol. 4</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Entries 002 and 003.

TWO entries from one source, deliberately. Bunge gives a general CHARACTERISATION
of system as an ordered triple, then explicitly says it is "not a definition
proper" and supplies a separate DEFINITION of concrete system. Collapsing them
into one entry would misrepresent him and would erase the caveat he himself
insisted on. The distinction is his, not ours.</rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <atlas:EvidenceCode rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PROP">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">propagated from another entry</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Asserted because a cited source restates another entry's definition. Inherits the strength of its source, never exceeds it.</skos:definition>
  </atlas:EvidenceCode>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/time">
    <skos:inScheme>
      <skos:ConceptScheme rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Systems-definition primitive vocabulary</rdfs:label>
        <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Controlled vocabulary of terms used as primitives in formal definitions of system. Deliberately flat and deliberately not an ontology of the world.</skos:definition>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">LEXICAL, NOT SEMANTIC. A primitive here records that an author uses a WORD as
a primitive. Two entries linked to prim:thing are not thereby claimed to mean the same
thing by it — Klir's "thing" is unrestricted, Bunge's ranges over concrete entities in
Theta, and whether those are one primitive or two is a census question this vocabulary
must not prejudge.

The consequence: recurrence counts computed over this scheme are a FIRST-PASS LEXICAL
measure. Semantic co-reference is a mapping-layer claim and owes evidence like any
other. Minting a separate primitive per author would make recurrence trivially zero;
merging them by hand would make it trivially high. Lexical recurrence is the honest
starting point, and its limits are stated here rather than discovered later.</skos:scopeNote>
      </skos:ConceptScheme>
    </skos:inScheme>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">time</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Bunge's triple is indexed at a time t. Klir's is not. A candidate point of divergence.</skos:scopeNote>
  </skos:Concept>
  <owl:AnnotationProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/displayForm">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">display form</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The span of the verbatim that is the definition's formal statement — the
face a reader meets first, e.g. `S = (T, R)` or `σ = ⟨C, E, S⟩`. Presentation only: it adds no
content, it marks a span. MUST be an exact substring of atlas:verbatim on the same individual —
the build refuses an entry where it is not, so this property can never put words in an author's
mouth. Absent where the definition has no formal statement to lift; the verbatim then faces
whole.</skos:definition>
  </owl:AnnotationProperty>
  <owl:AnnotationProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/includedExample">
    <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">included example</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">DEPRECATED 2026-08-03. Superseded by atlas:admits onto an atlas:Example. Retained declared so data written against the old shape stays readable.</rdfs:comment>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Something the author explicitly offers as a system on this definition.</skos:definition>
  </owl:AnnotationProperty>
  <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/instantiates">
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/Example"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/TestObject"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">instantiates</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Relates a case to the test object it is a case of. This is what makes a
disagreement derivable: same test object, one definition admitting and another refusing, is a
separating instance the build can find rather than one an encoder has to assert.</skos:definition>
  </owl:ObjectProperty>
  <owl:AnnotationProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/gloss">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">gloss</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Our description of a case, in our words. Distinct from atlas:verbatim on
the same individual, which is the author's. An example carries both: what the case is, and the
author's own reason for ruling on it as they did.</skos:definition>
  </owl:AnnotationProperty>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/entries/klir-2001-facets">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Atlas entry — Klir 2001, Facets of Systems Science</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Entry 001. The first entry in the catalogue, and the tradition the deck opens with.</rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/atlas-core">
    <owl:versionIRI rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/2026-08-01/atlas-core"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Definition Atlas — Core</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:license rdf:resource="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"/>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Scaffold for a catalogue of formal mathematical definitions of "system".

WHAT THIS ONTOLOGY ASSERTS: that certain DEFINITIONS exist, are borne by certain
documents, and invoke certain terms.

WHAT IT DOES NOT ASSERT: that the systems those definitions purport to define
exist. CCO's `is about` (cco:ont00001808) is deliberately left unasserted on
entries. This keeps the catalogue neutral between realist traditions (Bunge) and
constructivist ones (Klir), which is the whole point of the exercise: a merged or
realism-committed vocabulary would erase the very disagreements being catalogued.

WHY CCO: a definition is a generically dependent continuant that is about
something — exactly what BFO's GDC category and CCO's Information Content Entity
were introduced for. Alignment is therefore at the ANNOTATION layer only. The
per-tradition vocabularies (Klir's, Bunge's, Mobus's...) are the CONTENT of these
ICEs and are NOT re-parented as BFO domain ontologies.

TIER: this file supports tier 0 (bibliographic + verbatim + primitives invoked).
Tier 1 (dependency quiver) and above are not yet modelled here.

Spec: strategy/phd/definition-atlas-spec.md in the Halcyonic vault.</rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <owl:AnnotationProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/evidenceCode">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">evidence code</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Which atlas:EvidenceCode individual applies to this encoding.</skos:definition>
  </owl:AnnotationProperty>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/entries/bertalanffy-1972-history-status">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Atlas entry — Bertalanffy 1972, The History and Status of General Systems Theory</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Entry 004. The founder of the tradition, entered from a journal article rather
than the 1968 book — the first non-book bearer in the catalogue. The reason is transcription
discipline, recorded in the entry's apparatus.</rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <owl:AnnotationProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/arguedIn">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">argued in</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Where the claim carried by this term is argued — normally a document in mappings/, which owes a witness and must name its presentation.</skos:definition>
  </owl:AnnotationProperty>
  <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/statedIn">
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/FormalSystemDefinition"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://www.commoncoreontologies.org/ont00000253"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">stated in</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Relates a definition to the document that carries it.</skos:definition>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">TODO: align to the inverse of obo:BFO_0000101 (carrier of) once the CCO carrier chain has been read in full. Declared standalone for now rather than asserted wrongly.</rdfs:comment>
  </owl:ObjectProperty>
  <atlas:RetiredTerm rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/case/bunge-molecule-reef-family-factory">
    <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
    <dcterms:isReplacedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/case/bunge-molecule"/>
    <dcterms:isReplacedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/case/bunge-reef"/>
    <dcterms:isReplacedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/case/bunge-family"/>
    <dcterms:isReplacedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/case/bunge-factory"/>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Retired 2026-08-05: four cases were encoded as one individual, which blocked
each from ever being matched against another author's ruling on the same object. Split under
the P4 sign-off; first precedent under the D5 retirement policy (docs/iri-policy.md).</rdfs:comment>
  </atlas:RetiredTerm>
  <owl:AnnotationProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/sourceLocation">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">source location</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Where in the bearer the verbatim text appears — equation number, page, section.</skos:definition>
  </owl:AnnotationProperty>
  <atlas:EvidenceCode rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/MDU">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">model-drafted, unchecked</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A model produced the encoding and no human has checked it. MUST NOT be cited in any claim. Present so that coverage figures stay honest.</skos:definition>
  </atlas:EvidenceCode>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/imports/cco-bfo-reference">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">CCO/BFO reference module (minimal extract)</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Generated by build.py. Only the external terms the Definition Atlas actually uses, plus their named ancestors. Equivalent-class axioms, restrictions and disjointness from the source ontologies are NOT preserved -- this places terms and renders labels, it does not reproduce CCO/BFO semantics. Do not edit by hand.</rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <owl:AnnotationProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/encodedBy">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">encoded by</rdfs:label>
  </owl:AnnotationProperty>
  <owl:AnnotationProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/authorCaveat">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">author caveat</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A qualification the AUTHOR places on their own definition — that it is provisional, not a definition proper, too general, or holds only under stated conditions. Recorded verbatim. Suppressing these is how a catalogue starts misrepresenting its sources.</skos:definition>
  </owl:AnnotationProperty>
  <owl:AnnotationProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/verbatim">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">verbatim</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The definition exactly as written in the source, transcribed without paraphrase. The thing every downstream encoding must be checkable against.</skos:definition>
  </owl:AnnotationProperty>
  <owl:Ontology rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/definition-atlas"/>
  <owl:AnnotationProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/formalisedAs">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">formalised as</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The shape category in systems-science-foundations that encodes this
definition's dependency structure, as a path relative to that repository plus the
declaration name: `Systems/Category/ShapeKlir.lean#KlirShape`.</skos:definition>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">BIBLIOGRAPHIC, NOT A FAITHFULNESS CLAIM. This says only that a
formalisation of this definition exists and where to find it. Whether the quiver
is FAITHFUL to the passage -- which position encodes which primitive, which
phrase licenses which arrow, and what the encoding is silent about -- is a
mapping-layer claim and owes evidence like any other. Recording the pointer here
and the judgement there keeps the two from being confused.

DELIBERATELY A STRING, NOT AN IRI. The foundations are a separate repository with
its own release cadence; a resolvable IRI would pin this catalogue to a commit or
a hosting arrangement neither artifact controls. The reader resolves the path
against a configured checkout and fails the build if the declaration is absent.</rdfs:comment>
  </owl:AnnotationProperty>
  <owl:AnnotationProperty rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/encodedOn">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">encoded on</rdfs:label>
  </owl:AnnotationProperty>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000031">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">generically dependent continuant</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">(Elucidation) A generically dependent continuant is an entity that exists in virtue of the fact that there is at least one of what may be multiple copies which is the content or the pattern that multiple copies would share</skos:definition>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000002"/>
  </owl:Class>
  <skos:ConceptScheme rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/TestObjectScheme">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Test objects</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Objects that definitions of "system" rule on. Deliberately small: a term
is added only when the corpus contains at least two rulings on the same object, because a test
object with one ruling compares nothing and a test object with none is invented.</skos:definition>
  </skos:ConceptScheme>
  <atlas:TestObject rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/object/ordered-non-bonding">
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/TestObjectScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">an ordered but unconnected collection</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Members standing in an ordering, with no causal action between them: books
on a shelf by author, events in a sequence.

THE QUALIFIER IS LOAD-BEARING. Bunge refuses this case because ordering is not bonding, not
because the members are events. The refusal runs through his whole apparatus: an aggregate is
a collection "not held together by bonds" (ch. 1 p. 4), Definition 1.1 requires the members
CONNECTED — where a connection, unlike a mere relation "such as that of being older", has at
least one relatum acting on the other (Sec. 1.2) — and Definition 1.2(iii) carries the
nonempty-bondage condition 𝔅_A(σ,t) ≠ ∅ into every system's structure. A shelf of books
ordered by author carries a relation and no bonds. "Unconnected" in the label is Definition
1.1's own word; a plainer "an ordered collection" would understate the disagreement and invite
the reply that he was only excluding events.</skos:scopeNote>
    <atlas:evidenceCode rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/HVP"/>
    <atlas:encodedOn rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-08-05</atlas:encodedOn>
    <atlas:arguedIn>mappings/ordered-collection.md</atlas:arguedIn>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">HVP as of 2026-08-05: the identification was model-drafted (2026-08-03) and
has now been human-verified against both primary texts — Bunge's mere-relation/connection
distinction, Definition 1.1, its Example block and Definition 1.2(iii)'s 𝔅≠∅ condition read
against Klir p. 5's criterion and ordering passage. The verifying read confirmed that Klir's
admission is stated as a general principle ("any ordering of a set is a relation defined on
the set") and that Bunge's refusal is bond-absence, not member-kind. The derived conflict
inherits this grade.</rdfs:comment>
  </atlas:TestObject>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000002">
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000001"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">continuant</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">(Elucidation) A continuant is an entity that persists, endures, or continues to exist through time while maintaining its identity</skos:definition>
  </owl:Class>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000040">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">(Elucidation) A material entity is an independent continuant has some portion of matter as continuant part</skos:definition>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000004"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">material entity</rdfs:label>
  </owl:Class>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="https://www.commoncoreontologies.org/ont00000958">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Information Content Entity</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A Generically Dependent Continuant that generically depends on some Information Bearing Entity and stands in relation of aboutness to some Entity.</skos:definition>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000031"/>
  </owl:Class>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/FormalSystemDefinition">
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://www.commoncoreontologies.org/ont00000853"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Formal System Definition</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A Descriptive Information Content Entity that defines the
term "system" formally: it introduces named components with types or sorts, and
fixes at least one relation, function, or constraint among them, in symbols or in
prose precise enough to be transcribed to symbols without adding content.</skos:definition>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Inclusion criteria are stated in the spec, §5. Borderline cases are adjudicated there, not here.</rdfs:comment>
  </owl:Class>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/Example">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Example</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A case an author rules on: something they offer as a system, or refuse as
one. Reified rather than recorded as a string on the entry, because an example is a thing
several claims are made about -- where it appears in the source, how the encoding was
established, and which shared case it instantiates.</skos:definition>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">WHY THIS IS A CLASS. Examples were literal strings on the entry until
2026-08-03, which made four things impossible at once: an example could carry no evidence code
of its own, so one entry-level code graded a verbatim, five primitives and three examples
alike; no source location, though Bunge's examples sit outside the location his entry claims;
no separation of the case from the author's reason for it, concatenated with an em dash; and
no identity, so two authors ruling on the same object could not be matched. See
docs/proposals/P4-evidence-scope.md.</rdfs:comment>
  </owl:Class>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="https://www.commoncoreontologies.org/ont00000253">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">An Object upon which an Information Content Entity generically depends.</skos:definition>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000030"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Information Bearing Entity</rdfs:label>
  </owl:Class>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000030">
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000040"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">object</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">(Elucidation) An object is a material entity which manifests causal unity &amp; is of a type instances of which are maximal relative to the sort of causal unity manifested</skos:definition>
  </owl:Class>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">An idea or notion; a unit of thought.</skos:definition>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Concept</rdfs:label>
  </owl:Class>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/TestObject">
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Test Object</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">An object that definitions rule on, named once so that two authors ruling
on it can be identified as ruling on the SAME thing. Klir's "a collection of books ordered by
authors' names" and Bunge's "a collection of events, even if ordered" are two cases of one test
object; no string comparison will ever discover that.</skos:definition>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A TEST OBJECT IS ITSELF A CLAIM, and carries its own evidence code for that
reason. Asserting that two cases are about one object is an interpretation of both, made by
whoever draws the vocabulary — not something read off either source. Grading the object rather
than each link avoids a regress: the link inherits the identification's grade, and the
identification is argued in the mapping layer via atlas:arguedIn.

EXTERNAL AND PRIOR, like D1's candidate fix for the primitive scheme. A test object must be a
case an author actually ruled on, never one invented to make a comparison come out. If nothing
in the corpus rules on it, it does not belong here.</rdfs:comment>
  </owl:Class>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000004">
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000002"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">independent continuant</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">b is an independent continuant =Def b is a continuant &amp; there is no c such that b specifically depends on c or b generically depends on c</skos:definition>
  </owl:Class>
  <atlas:EvidenceCode rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/MDHC">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">model-drafted, human-checked</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A model produced the encoding; a human checked it against the verbatim text.</skos:definition>
  </atlas:EvidenceCode>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/EvidenceCode">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Evidence Code</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">How an encoding was established. Modelled on the Gene Ontology's evidence codes: every assertion carries a record of its provenance strength, and unverified assertions remain visible and filterable rather than silently equal to verified ones.</skos:definition>
  </owl:Class>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="https://www.commoncoreontologies.org/ont00000853">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Descriptive Information Content Entity</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">An Information Content Entity that consists of a set of propositions that describe some Entity.</skos:definition>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://www.commoncoreontologies.org/ont00000958"/>
  </owl:Class>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/RetiredTerm">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Retired Term</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A tombstone: an IRI this catalogue once served as a live term and will
never serve as one again. IRIs are permanent (w3id) — a term is never deleted and never
silently mutated; it is retired by CHANGING ITS CLASS to this one, so the strict shapes for
live terms keep their teeth and a stale citation still resolves to a signpost. A retired term
carries owl:deprecated true, at least one dcterms:isReplacedBy successor, and a comment saying
why. Policy and procedure: docs/iri-policy.md (D5). The reader's build refuses to serve a
deprecated IRI as live data, and that refusal is gated.</skos:definition>
  </owl:Class>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#ConceptScheme">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Concept Scheme</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A set of concepts, optionally including statements about semantic relationships between those concepts.</skos:definition>
  </owl:Class>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000001">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">entity</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">(Elucidation) An entity is anything that exists or has existed or will exist</skos:definition>
  </owl:Class>
</rdf:RDF>
